Let an ssh-ng client send its settings to a daemon that asks for them.

SSHStore::setOptions sends nothing (ssh-store.cc:81-88, an upstream TODO),
because a stock daemon applies the fixed header of SetOptions to every
client: a client's `max-jobs = 0` would stop the builder. So `--option`
never reaches a daemon over ssh-ng, pynixd included (nixidae/nixidae#1198).

The client names the handshake feature `set-options-by-name`. When the
daemon names it too:

- SSHStore sends SetOptions.
- Every overridden setting travels by name, the header ones included. The
  header cannot say "not set", so the daemon needs the names to tell a
  choice from a default. The user's nix.conf, NIX_CONFIG and `--option`
  mark a setting overridden; the system nix.conf does not
  (`loadConfFile` resets the marks after it).
- An unregistered setting named `pynixd-*` travels by name. Nix keeps such
  a name in `unknownSettings` and sends none of them otherwise. A second
  value for such a name replaces the first, as for a known setting.

The daemon decides which names it accepts from a remote client. A daemon
that does not name the feature sees what it sees today. 2.35 gates
SetOptions the other way on `disable-set-options`, for recursive Nix.

